Follow-up to aiecompiler_hostlib_fixed.log, which stopped at:
  ERROR: [aiecompiler 77-22139] Could not find the
  C:/Users/<user>/miniforge3/envs/ryzen-ai-1.7.1/Lib/site-packages/lib/win64.o/physical_device.dll
  file
after the hostlib wall cleared and the compiler got as far as
"Reading logical device aie2_5x4_device". This log answers two questions the
prior one left open: is physical_device.dll a literal filename or a
constructed/derivable one, and does any other AMD distribution on this
machine ship it.

Tooling added this session (neither was already installed):
  winget install --id 7zip.7zip --silent ...
  winget install --id activescott.lessmsi --silent ...
7-Zip opens the installer's PE+7z-SFX and lists/extracts its cabinets.
lessmsi is MSI-aware -- it reads the MSI's own File table to map the
opaque per-file names actually stored in each cabinet (e.g.
"flsulA7d1XEwok7Mk7kJOCruvai0_4") back to real filenames, and can extract
without running any install action (no services started, no registry
written, unlike a real msiexec install or the .exe's own /install path).

--- 1. Is "physical_device.dll" a literal name or built from a format string? ---

Binary-scanned all three copies of aiecompiler_client.dll present in the
ryzen-ai-1.7.1 pip env for ASCII runs of >=4 chars around every occurrence
of "physical_device" / "logical_device" (case-insensitive):
  Lib/site-packages/lib/win64.o/aiecompiler_client.dll            (132,767,824 bytes)
  Lib/site-packages/vaie_cpplus/lib/win64.o/aiecompiler_client.dll (10,092,624 bytes)
  Lib/site-packages/onnxruntime/capi/aiecompiler_client.dll        (same content as vaie_cpplus's copy)

First DLL, offset 0x763711e, one string-table run:
  ... | fildll | guidance_summary.dll | Dbghelp.dll | udm_api.dll |
  platform_device.dll | physical_device.dll | LLVMXLOptPreProc.dll | --dll | ...

Second and third DLLs (identical at this offset), offset 0x800370:
  The logical Device name %s is not supported. | physical_device.dll |
  createPhysicalDevice | setupComMsgMgr | The function 'createPhysicalDevice'
  is not found in the library. Please ...

Conclusion: "physical_device.dll" is a literal, hardcoded filename -- it
appears as a fixed string next to the runtime's own error messages, not
built via a %s pattern the way "The logical Device name %s is not
supported" is. The compiler expects to LoadLibrary this exact name and call
an exported createPhysicalDevice() from it. This is not something a
differently-named file already on disk could satisfy.

It also isn't alone. The same string table names a small family of
"physical backend" DLLs the compiler expects: physical_device.dll,
platform_device.dll, guidance_summary.dll, udm_api.dll. Checked all three
for existence anywhere in the ryzen-ai-1.7.1 pip env:
  find ... -iname platform_device.dll   -> no match
  find ... -iname guidance_summary.dll  -> no match
  find ... -iname udm_api.dll           -> no match
All three are absent, same as physical_device.dll. This isn't one missing
file; it's an entire class of physical-implementation backend components
that the redistributable SDK doesn't ship.

--- 2. Does the offline installer or the 1.8.0 SDK ship it instead? ---

C:\Users\<user>\Downloads\Downloads\ryzen-ai-lt-1.7.1.exe (2,749,950,720 bytes)
and ryzen-ai-1.8.0.exe (3,548,445,464 bytes) are full offline installers
already present on this machine (not downloaded for this investigation).
Both are a thin PE stub wrapping a solid 7z archive containing one .msi
plus ~20-22 numbered .cab files (the .msi's own File/Media tables map
files to cabs; cab entries use MSI's opaque per-file names, not real
filenames -- hence needing lessmsi, not just 7z, to identify anything).

ryzen-ai-lt-1.7.1.exe: extracted the SFX (2.7 GB, all 22 cabs) and ran
lessmsi's full extraction of ryzen-ai-lt-1.7.1.msi (1,961 files). The MSI
turns out to be nothing but the same 21 wheels this pip env already has,
byte-for-byte -- same version strings (vaie_overlay-1.7.1, vaie_cpplus-
1.7.1, device_essentials_strx_overlay-1.7.1, amd_quark-0.11rc1, etc.).
Opened vaie_overlay-1.7.1 and vaie_cpplus-1.7.1 (the two wheels that carry
aiecompiler_client.dll in the pip env) via `7z l` on the extracted .whl
files directly: no physical_device.dll, no platform_device.dll, no
guidance_summary.dll, no udm_api.dll in either. The offline installer is
not a different, more complete package -- it installs the identical pip
wheels via MSI instead of pip.

Also opened device_essentials_strx_overlay-1.7.1's own RECORD (dist-info
manifest, no extraction needed -- this wheel is already installed in the
pip env at Lib/site-packages/src/device_essentials_strx_overlay/): 189
files, all under data/FeatureStore/ and data/hierdb/optstrategy/*.pb (an
ML-based place-and-route congestion/timing feature store -- "FeatureStore",
"hierdb", "congestion_features.json", per-strategy model_X1.pb files) plus
the one entry already known from notes_aiecompiler_part_db.log:
  data/parts/xilinx/xclbin/strx/base.xclbin
No physical_device.dll and no phx equivalent of this package exists in the
1.7.1 wheel set at all (grepped every .whl name from the MSI's File table;
only "..._strx_overlay" appears, no "..._phx_overlay"). So the per-chip
physical-implementation DATA (feature store, congestion model) is shipped
for Strix only -- consistent with data/parts/xilinx/xclbin/ having only a
strx/ subdirectory (notes_aiecompiler_part_db.log) -- but even for Strix,
the physical_device.dll BACKEND CODE that would consume this data isn't
shipped either. Data and code are both incomplete, in different ways.

ryzen-ai-1.8.0.exe: extracted just the .msi (942 KB; this is the first
file in the solid 7z block so 7z didn't need to touch the 3.5 GB of
cabs behind it) and read its File table with lessmsi. The wheel set is
different from and non-overlapping with 1.7.1's aiecompiler-relevant
packages: aianalyzer, amd_atom, cardano, flexml (not flexml_lite), flexmlrt,
llvm_aie (not llvm_aie_lightweight), model_generate, onnxruntime_*,
ryzenai_dynamic_dispatch, ryzenai_onnx_utils, vaitrace, voe, waic --
1.8.0 (grep -i "physical_device"
against the full File table: no match; the search is conclusive without
needing the cabs, since the File table lists every filename regardless of
which cab holds it).

--- Conclusion ---

physical_device.dll (and its siblings platform_device.dll,
guidance_summary.dll, udm_api.dll) are real, hardcoded, expected filenames
-- not a derivable name a differently-named file could satisfy -- and they
are absent from every AMD distribution channel checked on this machine:
the ryzen-ai-1.7.1 pip package, the whole C: drive as loose files, the
ryzen-ai-lt-1.7.1.exe offline installer (byte-identical wheel content to
pip), and the ryzen-ai-1.8.0.exe offline installer (doesn't ship the
aiecompiler-bringup packages at all). Scoped claim, per CLAUDE.md: this is
"neither installer available on this machine, at either SDK version" --
not "no AMD distribution anywhere ships it." A full Vitis/Vivado install,
or a direct request to AMD, are the only remaining avenues, and both are
out of scope for this pass.

The shape of what's missing -- a physical place-and-route/timing backend,
paired with a per-chip ML congestion feature store that itself only covers
Strix in this SDK -- reads as a deliberate boundary in AMD's redistributable
packaging: pre-built xclbin overlays (base.xclbin, 1x4.xclbin, 4x4.xclbin)
ship to end users; the tool to build new ones from a hand-written ADF graph
for --target=hw does not. This closes the third and (for this pass) final
wall in the custom-kernel bring-up investigation.

Machine: Desktop 2 / Phoenix.
